<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hill of Tara</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hill of Tara is a low ridge in County Meath, Ireland, with prehistoric monuments including a passage tomb and earthworks. In early medieval Ireland it was the symbolic seat of the most prestigious kingship, and the title king of Tara, claimed especially by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-neill">Uí Néill</a>, came to mean high king of Ireland. Its monuments include the Mound of the Hostages and the Lia Fáil, the stone of destiny.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hill-of-tara.jpg" alt="Hill of Tara" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paulzygmunt · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Municipal_District_of_Ashbourne_-_Hill_of_Tara_-_2025-09-30_10-53-27_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their sanctuaries varied: timber temples within rectangular enclosures in Gaul, Belgica and Britain, ditched enclosures called viereckschanzen in southern Germany, and circular sites in Ireland, of which the <b>Hill of Tara</b> and Navan Fort are the most notable.</blockquote><a href="/religion/celtic-religion">Celtic religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Congal Cáech, king of the Ulaid and briefly claimant to the kingship of <b>Tara</b>, allied with Dál Riata but was defeated and killed by Domnall mac Áedo at Mag Rath in 637.</blockquote><a href="/state/dal-naraidi">Dal nAraidi</a></li><li><blockquote>The early Irish tale The Expulsion of the Déisi tells how they were driven from Brega near <b>Tara</b> and wandered south, some settling in Munster and others crossing to Dyfed in Wales, where the Kingdom of Dyfed's kings later claimed Déisi descent.</blockquote><a href="/state/deisi-muman">Deisi Muman</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingship of <b>Tara</b>, an ancient royal site in Meath, was claimed from the 6th century by the Ui Neill dynasties, who presented it as an ancient kingship of all Ireland.</blockquote><a href="/state/high-kingship-of-ireland">High Kingship of Ireland</a></li><li><blockquote>Brega's kings shared the kingship of <b>Tara</b> with Clann Cholmain and the northern Ui Neill, holding it often in the 7th and early 8th centuries and once more under Congalach Cnogba in the 10th.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-brega">Kingdom of Brega</a></li><li><blockquote>The Laigin, the people of Leinster, appear in the earliest Irish traditions as a major power whose kings once held the midlands and <b>Tara</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-leinster">Kingdom of Leinster</a></li></ul></section>
